    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Congress Park was born when Denver relocated graves from the old cemetery and turned the land into a public park in 1890.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congress_Park%2C_Denver",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood’s name comes from the park that replaced the former Capitol Hill cemetery, not from a congressional building.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congress_Park%2C_Denver",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1100 block of Elizabeth once housed Stevens School, now a condo landmark, illustrating adaptive reuse of historic buildings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.congressparkneighbors.org/history-of-congress-park-neighborhood/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Capitol Heights Presbyterian Church, founded in a tent in 1895, once stood on 11 Ave and Fillmore and remains a historic landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://history.denverlibrary.org/neighborhood-history-guide/congress-park",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area that became Congress Park evolved into Cheesman Park, Denver Botanic Gardens, and the Water Board Reservoirs over a century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
